From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1vivy <1vivy@tutanota.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 02:39:14 -0600
Subject: Worker: pluggable channel factory

Worker.start() always calls Channel.open(), so a worker can only run in
an isolate (VM) or a Web Worker (browser). Channel is already an
interface, but the worker's channel is private and Channel.open() is not
injectable, so a custom Channel implementation has no way in. Running
the same service in another process (an elevated helper reached over a
loopback socket, say) means forking Squadron or re-implementing what
Worker does.

Add ChannelFactory and Worker.channelFactory (constructor parameter and
settable field, documented as "must be set before the worker starts").
When set, the first request awaits the factory instead of
Channel.open(); everything after it is unchanged: stats, the shared
_openChannel future (concurrent first requests open the channel once)
and stop(). A factory's error reaches the caller the way a failing
Channel.open() does. With no factory, behavior is identical to before,
and no existing API changes. The factory signature follows the
Channel.open() parameters without PlatformThreadHook, because a custom
channel has no platform thread; the thread hook is not called for
channels opened this way.

Export SquadronCancelationToken and StreamId, which a Channel
implementation needs for cancelToken() and cancelStream().

The hook is small and generic: any transport that can carry Squadron's
request/response lists (a socket, a native port, an in-memory test
double) becomes a place a worker can run, without Squadron knowing
about it.

Tests: test/13_channel_factory_suite.dart, in Squadron's TestContext
style and registered in both the VM and the browser suite: a factory
that decorates Channel.open(), a factory that replaces the channel with
one that has no platform thread behind it, one open for concurrent
first requests, factory errors reaching the caller, and the default
path.

Checks run:
- 2026-10-01, on v7.4.4 (4ab7f15a): VM suite 694 passed; browser suite
  (Chrome, dart2js and dart2wasm, JS and Wasm workers) 2940 passed.
  Squadron's existing analyzer infos are unchanged; the patch adds none.
- On fd5becc (7.4.5) as the only commit (fork branch
  worker-channel-factory @ 493829dc), Dart 3.13.7, Chrome
  157.0.8103.50: `dart analyze` reports no issues;
  `dart test -p vm test/squadron_vm_test.dart` 694 passed, 5 skipped;
  after `dart run tool/compile_tests.dart js`,
  `dart test -p chrome --compiler dart2js test/squadron_browser_test.dart`
  735 passed, 5 skipped. The five CHANNEL FACTORY tests pass in both
  runners.
- 2026-11-17, this series on fd5becc, Dart 3.13.4:
  `dart test -p vm test/squadron_vm_test.dart` 694 passed, 5 skipped.
  Browser runs of that rebase are the ones listed above.

Rebase notes: carried unchanged from v7.4.4 (4ab7f15a) to fd5becc; only
the CHANGELOG context moved under the 7.4.5 entry. fd5becc is the
published 7.4.5 (pub.dev's lib/ and pubspec.yaml match it; upstream has
no tag for it) and, on 2026-11-20, still the latest stable release and
the head of main.

